From 4c81f3f711a50e51fb4a03af28b49170c772f90a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 00:33:36 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(repair): park a refused base refresh for the owner and
 keep its PR bound (JAM B11)

Sentinel issue 157 was absorbing. R79 (549c7a691) implemented,
preserved and published 784e233ec at d5cc84402 as PR 160; the candidate
edits ops/hosted-autonomy-follow-ups.ts,
ops/hosted-autonomy-follow-up-pass.ts and
ops/hosted-autonomy-github-issues.ts, which R80 (e48e7e110, installed
minutes later) added to the owner-protected paths. When
development moved to 614a4fa02, R80 regenerated the deterministic refresh
merge 18cceb957 and the trusted publication validator refused it: "a
protected entry does not match the trusted base tree" (reproduced
byte-identically offline; R79's validator accepts both heads, R80's
refuses both). The refusal is correct policy. The defects were around it:
R80 counted that deterministic `invalid` verdict as a transient failure,
re-ran the same refresh twice more over 70 minutes, dropped the reason,
and blocked through markTerminalBlocked, which cleared target.pr and left
PR 160 bound to no record. The re-arm pass then reported
`refresh:issue-ubiquity-sentinel-157:retained:activation` on every pass:
a refusal at or after the corrected validator's activation is never
re-armed, so nothing could move it and nothing named it for the owner.

Runtime (src/, takes effect with the next runtime generation):
- The first deterministic preservation verdict of an unmarked refresh
  (`invalid` with one of DETERMINISTIC_PUBLICATION_DETAILS) parks the
  record at once: "base refresh refused by trusted validation; owner
  action required (land the change by hand or close the issue): <reason>",
  the reason being "candidate touches a protected path" for a
  protected-entry verdict and the closed validator detail otherwise. A
  malformed request and a marked conflict-resolution refresh keep the
  bounded failure accounting.
- The park and the bounded-failure block keep the still-open pull request
  as target.pr (markBlocked, not markTerminalBlocked). Identity
  contradictions and closed publications still clear it.

Launcher (ops/, correct under R80):
- The refresh re-arm pass parks an R80-written refusal at or after the
  activation (or with no provable activation) in one CAS write: the same
  prefix with the original message after it, and the PR binding restored
  from the same native publication facts a re-arm verifies. An
  unverified publication parks without a binding and is bound by one
  later write once it verifies; a number is never guessed.
- Every park, launcher- or runtime-written, is named on every later pass
  (`refresh:<id>:owner_required:pr=<n|unverified>`), a bound park with
  no read or write. No retry, re-admission, CI or delivery rule matches
  the prefix.
  Nothing is charged, refunded, pushed, reviewed or merged; no pull
  request is closed and no branch deleted.

Live replay of the full repair state (290 records) through the real
maintenance pass: the live launcher reports `retained:activation` on
every pass; this launcher writes only issue 157 on its first pass
(`owner_parked:pr=160`) and names it on every pass after. Every new
shape parses under R80's own parser, and R80's own selection skips it.

